**Verdict:** The D-VS2 entry at [$80,372](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/18152689?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is an unusual situation — D color at the entry price is rare in any size. If you are buying natural 4ct, start there. The F-VVS1 at [$110,661](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27292860?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the quality sweet spot — F color and VVS1 clarity for [$30,289](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27292860?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) more than the entry, which is a reasonable premium for a significantly better certificate at this weight.

> **Farzana on Natural 4ct:**
>
> Most buyers at this level want to know if D-VS2 is a good natural entry at [$80,372](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/18152689?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). It is — D color at the lowest available price in 4ct certified inventory is not common. That said, VS2 at 4ct radiant needs individual confirmation that it is eye-clean. Request the video before you buy.
>
> If you are asking whether natural 4ct is worth it at all when lab delivers the same 10mm stone for [$7,891](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27493916?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — that is a question about rarity versus price, not quality versus price. Both are real diamonds. At 4ct, only one of them is genuinely rare.

